<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Council – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Share Our Voice in the Community</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present an analysis of a current issue, local or global. Interactive: Engage in discussions of general or academic interests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present an analysis of civic responsibilities, prompting classmates to share their views on community engagement (e.g., "My analysis shows civic responsibility improves community well-being. What do you think?"). Can engage in role-play discussions simulating town council meetings to present key points and respond to community suggestions (e.g., "The new park renovations were approved based on citizen feedback. What other suggestions should be implemented?"). Can summarize classmates' ideas during discussions on local governance, keeping the conversation focused (e.g., "We all agree that voting is a key responsibility of citizens.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various community activities (e.g., a park clean-up, a public meeting, people voting). Students work in pairs to discuss: "What responsibilities do citizens have in these pictures? How do these people share their voice in the community?" The teacher encourages the use of vocabulary like 'civic', 'participation', 'involvement', 'community', 'vote'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences for expressing opinions and asking questions about civic duties, using target grammar. For example: "Citizens <i>must</i> participate in elections to elect their authorities. What do you think?" or "Community projects <i>are often decided</i> based on citizen feedback. Do you agree?" The teacher pays attention to the pronunciation of /ɪ/ in 'civic' and 'public', /e/ in 'share', and word stress in 'responsibility' and 'participation'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'town council'? Is it a group that makes decisions for the community or a place for sports?" (Checks understanding of 'town council' and 'decisions').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'Citizens should express their views,' am I talking about their responsibilities or their hobbies?" (Checks understanding of 'express' and 'responsibilities'.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short (1-2 minute) simulated dialogue from a local news report or a podcast segment about a recent town council meeting. The dialogue features a reporter interviewing a citizen and a council member about a community issue (e.g., "The new park renovations were approved based on citizen feedback."). It includes examples of passive voice, reported speech, and modals related to civic duties and community decisions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engages in discussions of general or academic interests, presenting key points and responding to suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Students listen once and discuss in pairs: "What was the main topic of the town council meeting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a second time and match statements to speakers (reporter, citizen, council member). E.g., "Said that new policies would be implemented soon." / "Expressed concern about public services."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen a third time and decide if statements about the dialogue are true or false. E.g., "Decisions are made without public involvement." (False)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using passive voice, reported speech, and modals ("can," "should," "must") to discuss civic responsibilities and community issues, focusing on clear articulation and appropriate tone. Target vocabulary: 'town council', 'community', 'voice', 'feedback', 'decisions', 'participation', 'civic duties', 'responsibilities', 'public services', 'involved', 'express', 'inform', 'recommend'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation:</b> Students receive cards with active sentences or direct quotes related to civic matters. In pairs, they transform them into passive voice or reported speech verbally. E.g., Teacher gives: "The citizens reported the issue." Students say: "The issue was reported by the citizens." Teacher gives: "A resident said, 'We need better public services.'" Students say: "A resident said that they needed better public services."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Civic Opinion Chain:</b> The teacher provides a list of civic actions (e.g., "participate in elections," "attend town council meetings," "provide feedback on projects," "report problems"). One student states an action using a modal ("Citizens <i>should</i> attend town council meetings."). The next student must respond with agreement or disagreement and add another modal, explaining why, or introduce a new action. E.g., "I agree, citizens <i>must</i> be informed. They <i>can</i> also provide feedback on projects." Focus on pronunciation of /ɪ/ and /e/ + r sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Civic Priorities" Discussion:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a list of community issues (e.g., public safety, local park renovations, waste management, youth programs). They must discuss and prioritize three issues, explaining their choices using phrases like "In my opinion, X <i>should be prioritized</i> because..." or "The mayor <i>said that</i> X <i>might be</i> important." They practice asking for opinions ("What do you think are important duties for citizens?") and showing respect ("Could you tell us more about your ideas?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 5px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play simulation: "Our Town Council Meeting".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups (4-5 students). Each group represents a 'Town Council Committee' or 'Concerned Citizen Group'. They are given a specific local issue (e.g., "Traffic Congestion in the Town Center," "Lack of Green Spaces," "Need for Youth Programs").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students first prepare a brief (2-3 minute) analysis of their assigned issue, including background, proposed solutions, and a call for community involvement. They must use passive voice, reported speech, and modals from the list (e.g., "The problem <i>has been discussed</i> in previous meetings," "Residents <i>said that</i> public transportation <i>should be improved</i>," "Citizens <i>must contribute</i> their voice.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the 'meeting', each group presents its analysis. After each presentation, other groups (acting as other committees or citizens) ask questions, offer suggestions, and share their viewpoints. Students must engage in respectful dialogue, ask for opinions ("What do you think are important duties for citizens?"), respond to suggestions using formal language ("Thank you for sharing your thoughts on our community."), and attempt to summarize key ideas ("We all agree that voting is a key responsibility of citizens.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, use of target language, and task completion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the role-play simulation from Stage 4. Each student's participation will be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of /ɪ/, /e/ + r, and word stress in multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (nouns: 'town council', 'feedback', 'decisions', 'responsibilities'; verbs: 'participate', 'express', 'inform', 'recommend'; adjectives: 'civic', 'responsible', 'involved').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct and natural use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals ('can', 'should', 'must').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in presenting analysis and sharing viewpoints.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective engagement in discussions (asking for opinions, responding respectfully, summarizing ideas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of formal language and respectful tone in public dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick written reflection at the end of the lesson.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I shared about community involvement today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One grammatical structure (passive voice, reported speech, or modals) I used effectively today was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-6 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the town council meeting role-play did you find most challenging when speaking, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using phrases for asking opinions or showing respect help you communicate better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The role-play activity was very engaging, and students were able to use the target grammar and vocabulary productively. Some students still need more practice with reported speech, specifically with tense changes. The pronunciation of word stress in longer words like 'responsibility' will require ongoing practice. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence starters for summarizing classmates' ideas to ensure the conversation remains focused. Scaffolding could include pre-prepared note cards for the role-play with key phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson has prepared students to articulate their thoughts on civic issues. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on this by having students write a formal letter or proposal to the town council, using the same grammar structures and vocabulary to formally express their views and suggest solutions in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3-5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>